<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS hardened-runtime entitlements for the Bun-compiled engine SIDECAR
  (Contents/MacOS/sovrium in the bundle), and for nothing else. The app shell
  is signed with shell.entitlements.plist, which holds none of these keys.

  Why allow-jit is here: MEASURED, not recommended. On 2026-10-29, on macOS
  27.0.1 (Apple Silicon) with the Bun 1.4.4 engine signed with the hardened
  runtime, the engine with NO entitlement at all launched, served, and passed
  the desktop smoke test; JavaScriptCore simply ran without its JIT, and a
  CPU-bound script took about seven times as long (roughly 7 to 8 seconds
  against 1). allow-jit ALONE restored the full speed, and also passed the
  smoke test. So allow-jit is kept because the engine is unusably slow
  without it, not because macOS kills it at launch: it does not. The record
  and the protocol are ADR-036 A3, which a change to this file updates.

  The four other keys Bun lists for a standalone executable
  (https://bun.com/docs/bundler/executables, "Code signing on macOS") were
  DROPPED on 2026-10-30, on the founder's decision (ADR-036 A3): that
  measurement found no use for any of them, and two of them,
  disable-library-validation and allow-dyld-environment-variables, let code
  not signed by our team into this process. They were
  allow-unsigned-executable-memory, disable-executable-page-protection,
  allow-dyld-environment-variables and disable-library-validation. Do not
  restore one without a measurement that needs it, recorded in ADR-036.
  desktop/tauri-cli.ts refuses a signed build whose staged engine carries
  anything but allow-jit, and both macOS build workflows assert the same set.

  Who applies this file. The release and desktop build workflows sign the staged
  sidecar with it, before the bundle is built. Tauri would then sign that same
  file AGAIN with the bundle-wide entitlements (it forces a new signature on
  every external binary), which is why desktop/tauri-cli.ts hands the sidecar
  to the bundler as a plain file rather than as an external binary on macOS:
  the signature made with this file is the one that ships. The same script
  refuses a signed build whose staged sidecar does not carry allow-jit.

  NOTHING IN THIS COMMENT MAY CONTAIN TWO CONSECUTIVE HYPHENS. An XML comment is
  not allowed to, and the parser that enforces it is AMFI, inside codesign. The
  line above said "a `-`+`-`deep signature" until 2026-09-22, and the result was
  that EVERY signed build failed with `Failed to parse entitlements:
  AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 15`, a message that names this
  file's line number and says nothing about hyphens. Spell such a flag out in
  words instead.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
